  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 340 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nA passing clipper system in Canada will bring a weak yet mainly\ndry cold front across the forecast area today. Look for some\nbreezy northwest winds as a result. Some cooler temperatures in\nthe 60s will be found in the north behind this front today, with\n70s elsewhere ahead of this front. As mentioned this front is\nexpected to be mainly dry. There could be a few showers near the\ninternational border, yet confidence was too low to include at\nthis time. Surface high will fully push this cold front through\ntonight. Look for cooler lows in the mid 30s to mid 40s tonight\nas a result. Perhaps some areas of frost are possible, especially\nin the north. Surface high then settles in for Thursday, with\nthe cold front creating a stalled boundary in the southwest. A\nfew showers are possible along this southwestern boundary,\nalthough there is an abundance of dry air these showers would\nhave to go through. For now have low to medium pops to account\nfor this potential. Otherwise look for cooler temperatures on\nThursday with highs in the mid 50s to mid 60s, and less breezy\nnorthwest winds. Surface high moves more over central ND\nThursday night into Friday morning. The mentioned boundary could\nstill remained stalled in the southwest, perhaps lingering low\nchances for showers. With this high will be cooler temperatures\nThursday night into Friday morning with lows forecast in the 30s\nto lower 40s. This could bring more widespread frost \nconditions. The surface high moves out of the state on Friday, \nswitching the flow aloft to the southwest. Developing surface \nfront to our west will also bring a breezy southerly flow for \nFriday. As a result and elevated warm front looks to move \nthrough Friday, followed by a weak clipper system Friday night. \nLow to medium chances for showers could be found from either of \nthese features. Temperatures could warm slightly, yet overall \nremain in the 60s for highs. Cloudy conditions could also bring \nslightly warmer temperatures Friday night into Saturday \nmorning, with lows forecast in the mid 30s to mid 40s.\n\nSome lingering showers could be found Saturday morning as the\nmentioned clipper system moves through. The rest of Saturday\nthen looks near seasonable with highs in the 60s. Some breezy\nnorthwest winds could also be found for Saturday. Lows in the\n30s could return Saturday night into Sunday morning, with high\npressure keeping conditions dry. This high also looks to keep\nconditions dry for Sunday, while temperatures remain about the\nsame with highs in the 60s. Zonal flow for early next week could\nbring weak disturbances and low pops at times to start next\nweek. This westerly flow could also bring some warming\ntemperatures, with forecast highs currently in the mid 60s to\nmid 70s Monday and Tuesday. Milder morning lows would also be\nfound with this pattern. An increased gradient at the surface\ncould also bring some breezy southerly winds on Monday.",
